Patch 60 (dmsynth p35) -- self-heal a stalled synth sink transport.

The synth sink's timing thread starts the DirectSound buffer once with
DSBPLAY_LOOPING and then waits on the notification event with INFINITE.  Every
play/write position the render thread reads comes from that one loop.  If the
transport stops, or the notifications stop arriving, the thread stays alive but
blocked for good: sink->play_pos never advances again, the render thread keeps
writing into a buffer nothing plays, and there is no path back short of
restarting the game.  That matches the reported defect -- gameplay SFX gone
after the console suspends and resumes, and less often after a death or a
transition, with music and menus unaffected in some reports.

Wait with a timeout instead and use the expiry as a watchdog tick.  Two
different failures are handled, because DirectSound reports them differently:

  * The buffer is stopped or lost: Restore if needed, then Play with
    DSBPLAY_LOOPING again.  This is the documented recovery path.
  * The buffer still reports DSBSTATUS_PLAYING while its play position does not
    advance.  Wine keeps that flag for the secondary buffer itself regardless of
    what the mixer and the endpoint below it are doing, so a device that stopped
    advancing across a suspend is invisible in the status word.  After
    MGS2_DMSYNTH_WATCHDOG_STALL consecutive ticks on an unchanged play position,
    the transport is re-armed once with Stop + Play.

This is not polling.  While notifications arrive -- BUFFER_SUBDIVISIONS per
buffer, 200 ms apart at this sink's format -- the loop is woken by the event
exactly as before and the timeout does not expire.  In the broken state it costs
one GetStatus per tick and logs nothing per tick, so rule 2 is respected.  A
failed recovery is deliberately not fatal and only the first failure of an
episode is reported: ending the thread there would guarantee the silence the
watchdog exists to undo.

Two lifecycle details, both from review:  the stop event is re-checked with a
zero timeout before the transport is touched, because a stop request can land
while the wait was timing out and the loop would otherwise restart a transport
the sink is already tearing down; and a successful re-arm clears both the stall
count and the last-seen position, so a recovery cannot trigger the next one from
the very position it was recovering from.

sink->written is deliberately not touched from the timing thread.  The render
thread already resynchronises it against the published play position in its own
underrun check, so a re-armed transport is caught up by the thread that owns
that field instead of racing it from this one.

Knobs, read once per process:

  MGS2_DMSYNTH_WATCHDOG_MS      watchdog period, default 250, 0 restores the
                                exact pre-patch INFINITE wait -- so the control
                                arm is this same binary with one variable set
  MGS2_DMSYNTH_WATCHDOG_STALL   consecutive stalled ticks before a re-arm,
                                default 4, 0 keeps the watchdog and disables the
                                position-stall re-arm

Status: built as binaries/dmsynth_p35_resume_recover.dll
(f387ff2d2f0273deee4313442c03c51373dc1ecaae3134c33cafde1a56392d0c), semantically
verified to change only synth_sink_timing_thread (458 -> 625 instructions, no
function added or removed), and NOT yet measured on the device.
The status-only half is the documented DirectSound recovery; the stall re-arm is
reasoned from Wine's dsound keeping its own PLAYING flag and has no capture of a
resumed device behind it yet.

